<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Jul 8, 2022">
<CPscript title="Harmonic Measure" level="min" screenmode="map" >
<description>In this script we investigate the notion of discrete harmonic measure and some connections with random walks and the packing algorithm.</description>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" iconname="netbeans.png">act 1;Cleanse;infile_read randbase.p;set_brush 0;geom_to_e;infile_path plate.g;Disp -w -t0fc5t1 A -g;Map 0 1; </cmd>
<text> We consiider a homogeneous thin metal plate in the shape of the region G in p1 and with a designated interior point taken as the origin. We wish to model steady state temperature distributions as we hold some boundary arc at temperature 100 while the rest of the boundary is held at temperature 0. 

Classically, this is solved using conformal mappings. To get our discrete approximation, we have cut this shape out of a geometrically random triangulatioin. (This is done with 'cookie', but I've saved the result to avoid any complications.) </text>
<cmd iconname="klines.png">Disp -p1 -w -c -t0fc5t1 A -g; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="kghostview.png">infile_read cutout_1.p;set_brush 0;Disp -p1 -w -c -t0fc5t1 A -g; </cmd>
<Section title="Eyeballs and Estimates">
<text> We mark three arcs: 'A' is green, 'B' is blue, and 'C' is red, in the boundary of our region. They all have roughly the same arclength, but different harmonic effects at the origin.

QUESTION: Which arc do you believe has the greatest harmonic measure at the origin?

The is essentially the question: when an arc is held at 100, the rest of the boundary at 0, what is the temperature at the origin (after waiting for the temperature to stabilize)? </text>
<cmd iconname="metacontact_online.png">act 1;[A];[B];[C] </cmd>
<text> Let us consider the answer for 'A' first. We compute the conformal map from the disc, using the maximal packing in the domain, p0. (It is convenient to convert this to a euclidean packing.) </text>
<cmd iconname="icq_dnd.png">act 0;set_brush 0;copy -p1 0;set_brush 0;max_pack 2000;geom_to_e;Disp -w -c -u -t0fc5t1 A;[A]; </cmd>
<text> The temperature will clearly be quite low --- perhaps less than 1 degree? The discrete harmonic measure of the arc 'A' at the origin (with respect to G) is the arclength measure of the preimage of 'A' under the conformal map (when the designated point is the image of the origin).

If we mark the preimages of arcs 'B' and 'C' as well, you can now judge which has the largest harmonic measure. How did your intuition hold up? </text>
<cmd iconname="format_increaseindent.png">act 0;disp -wr;[A];[B];[C]; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Extension of Domain">
<text> There is a fascinating connection between harmonic measure and the probability topic known as "Brownian motion". In the discrete setting the connection is with random walks. We cannot go into details here, but consider a random walker starting at the origin and randomly moving from circle to circle in our packing. The walker will almost certainly run into the boundary at some point. The harmonic measure of arc 'A' is essentially the same as the probability that the random walker will first hit the boundary in the arc 'A' instead of in its complement.

This probabilistic connection can be a great aide to the intuition, since humans seem to have some inate sense of chance and probabilities. An example is the extension of domain principle.

In p2 we have "extended" the region G by cookie'ing a slightly bigger region G'. The origin and the arc 'C' are unaffected, and we mark them as before. </text>
<cmd iconname="editdelete.png">act 2;infile_read cutout_2.p;set_brush 0;infile_path bigger.g;cookie;Disp -w -c -t0fc5t1 A -g;[C];Map 1 2; </cmd>
<text> QUESTION: Observing these two regions, G and G'. Is the harmonic measure of 'C' larger in G or larger in G'?

I'm going to let you figure out the answer; you can do this by mapping to the unit disc with this new region as before. 

However, I suggest that you first do a thought experiment with random walkers: compare their walk in the packings for G and G'. What does this suggest about the likelihood that they will exit on 'C'? </text>
<cmd iconname="klines.png">act 1;max_pack 10000;geom_to_e;Disp -w -c -u -t0fc5t1 A;[C];act 2;max_pack 10000;geom_to_e;Disp -w -c -u -t0fc5t1 A;[C]; </cmd>
<text> The comparison here, between the harmonic measure of an arc in the boundary of two nested regions, is rather difficult to see here, but the red arc in p2 is larger than in p1. This is the content of the "Extension of Domain Principle". </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Random Visitations">
<text> The random walkers discussed in the previous section are connected not only to harmonic measure but also to the circle packing algorithm. We investigate that here using arc 'C'. We start with copies of the packing of G in both p0 and p1. </text>
<cmd iconname="list.png">act 0;infile_read cutout_1.p;set_brush 0;infile_path plate.g;set_screen -d;Disp -w -c -t0fc5t1 A -g;[C];act 0;copy 1;act 1;set_screen -d;Disp -w -c -t0fc5t1 A -g;[C];Map 0 1 </cmd>
<text> The thought experiment is now as follows: Suppose all the circles defining arc 'C' in the circle packing of G have their radii increased by some (small) factor. To reestablish a circle packing, the packing algorithm has to be run, and the changes on 'C' propogate to all the interior radii.

QUESTION: How do you think those adjustments on the inside will be distributed? 

The next commands carry out such adjustments in p1 in three stages: adjust 'C', repack and show interior radii, then layout. Disregard the layout, our interest is really in the radii. </text>
<cmd iconname="debugger.png">act 1;adjust_rad 1.5 b(1366 1383);disp -cfc195 b(1366 1383); </cmd>
<cmd iconname="delete.png">act 1;repack;disp -c -cf120 b(1366 1383); </cmd>
<cmd iconname="network.png">act 1;layout;disp -wr;[C]; </cmd>
<text> So, what is the punchline? The next command compares the radii pf p0 to the adjusted radii in p1 and color codes them: red circles are larger in p1 than in p0. It is difficult to tell, but all of them are red; increasing the boundary required increases in all interior circles. </text>
<cmd iconname="run.png">act 0;color -p1 -c q0 a;get_data -q1 -c a;disp -wr;disp -cf; </cmd>
<text> Also, the larger the percentage increase in its radius in p1, the more red.the circle in p0. The red is actually the distribution of steady state temperature! And the connection to our original harmonic measure question? The factor by which the circle at the origin was increased converts roughly to the temperature there.

A final observation. You may think of random walkers as launched from the arc 'C' and wandering around the interior until they exit somewhere on the boundary. The redness of an interior circle reflects how many random walkers visited that circle. So, as your intuition might suggest, the changes on 'C' work their way throughout the packing, but their influence diminishes the further you are (harmonically) from 'C'. </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="A" mnemonic="A" tooltip="display green arc" iconname="kghostview.png">set_Elist b(1400 1421);disp -ec233t10 Elist; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="B" mnemonic="B" tooltip="display blue arc" iconname="amarok.png">set_Elist b(1288 1303);disp -ec5t10 Elist; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="C" mnemonic="C" tooltip="display red arc" iconname="apollon.png">set_Elist b(1366 1383);disp -ec195t10 Elist; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
